        {
          "id": "bfa97575-5ee5-30bd-80cf-7ea5de293e1b",
          "content": {
            "id": "bfa97575-5ee5-30bd-80cf-7ea5de293e1b",
            "contentType": "VIDEO",
            "title": "BP profit beats expectations, helped by refining margins",
            "description": "<title><body><p>STORY: BP reported a smaller than expected fall in third-quarter underlying profit Tuesday (November 4).</p><p>The energy giant had to face a fall in oil prices during the period.</p><p>Average Brent crude prices were down 13% from the same period last year, but higher refining margins partly offset the impact of this for BP.</p><p>The company said it made an underlying replacement cost profit, or adjusted net income, of $2.21 billion.</p><p>That was above analyst forecasts and marginally below last year's number.</p><p>BP's European rivals Shell and TotalEnergies also posted third-quarter profit falls last month as they were likewise\u00a0dragged down by lower oil prices.</p><p>BP kept the pace of its quarterly share buyback programme at $750 million through the third quarter.</p><p>Chief Executive Murray Auchincloss said he expects completed or announced asset sale agreements would reach around $5 billion this year.</p><p>There was no update on the closely-watched sale process for BP's Castrol lubricants unit, which\u00a0is central to its $20 billion disposal programme.</p><p>BP has vowed to improve profitability and cut costs while spending more on oil and gas.</p><p>That follows a drive into renewables under its last CEO Bernard Looney.</p><p>BP's customers and products division was boosted by higher refining margins.</p><p>It reported profit before interest and tax of $1.61 billion, well ahead of last year's $381 million.</p><p>BP shares were up close to 1% in early trade - outperforming a broader index of European energy companies.</p></body></title>",
            "summary": "<body><p>STORY: BP reported a smaller than expected fall in third-quarter underlying profit Tuesday (November 4).</p><p>The energy giant had to face a fall in oil prices during the period.</p><p>Average Brent crude prices were down 13% from the same period last year, but higher refining margins partly offset the impact of this for BP.</p><p>The company said it made an underlying replacement cost profit, or adjusted net income, of $2.21 billion.</p><p>That was above analyst forecasts and marginally below last year's number.</p><p>BP's European rivals Shell and TotalEnergies also posted third-quarter profit falls last month as they were likewise&nbsp;dragged down by lower oil prices.</p><p>BP kept the pace of its quarterly share buyback programme at $750 million through the third quarter.</p><p>Chief Executive Murray Auchincloss said he expects completed or announced asset sale agreements would reach around $5 billion this year.</p><p>There was no update on the closely-watched sale process for BP's Castrol lubricants unit, which&nbsp;is central to its $20 billion disposal programme.</p><p>BP has vowed to improve profitability and cut costs while spending more on oil and gas.</p><p>That follows a drive into renewables under its last CEO Bernard Looney.</p><p>BP's customers and products division was boosted by higher refining margins.</p><p>It reported profit before interest and tax of $1.61 billion, well ahead of last year's $381 million.</p><p>BP shares were up close to 1% in early trade - outperforming a broader index of European energy companies.</p></body>",
